## The Challenge
When I first read the challenge, I saw a long string of numbers like `0x70 0x69 0x63 0x6f ...`. The description said:
> The ‘0x’ prefix in front of these numbers tells you that the number is in hexadecimal base. 
You could look up an ASCII table and solve this problem manually, but I would recommend looking at the hints for this problem for a more efficient solution.
I knew `0x` meant hexadecimal. I also knew ASCII maps numbers to characters. But I had no idea how many characters there were, and I didn't want to manually convert 50+ hex values. That sounded like torture.

## First Thoughts – "This Should Be Easy"
I thought, *"Okay, hex to ASCII. I can just use Python."* So I opened my terminal and tried:

```python
>>> hex_string = "0x70 0x69 0x63 0x6f ..."
>>> bytes.fromhex(hex_string).decode()

I'm not exaggerating when I say I tried at least 15 different things. Here's a rough log of my chaos:

Manual ASCII table – I converted the first few: 0x70 = p, 0x69 = i, 0x63 = c, 0x6f = o. I got pico. Then I looked at the rest and thought, "Nope, not doing this for 50 more."

Python with bytes.fromhex() – Failed because of 0x and spaces.

Python with replace('0x', '') – Still failed because of spaces.
Python with replace(' ', '') – Finally got a hex string, but then I realized I needed to remove 0x too.

Python with both replaces – It worked! But I accidentally ran it in a fresh terminal and lost the string. I had to re-copy everything.

Tried binascii.unhexlify() – Same issues.
Tried xxd -r -p – Forgot that xxd expects plain hex without 0x or spaces. Got garbage.

Tried echo ... | xxd -r -p – Still garbage because of the 0x prefixes.

Tried CyberChef – I went to the website, pasted the whole string, and looked for "From Hex". But I pasted it with 0x and spaces. It didn't work. I thought CyberChef was broken.

